1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,290
Outlook and Teams just went down

2
00:00:02,290 --> 00:00:03,250
around the world.

3
00:00:03,250 --> 00:00:06,191
And Microsoft says it was not an attack.

4
00:00:06,241 --> 00:00:10,081
On November twelfth, Microsoft 365 started

5
00:00:10,081 --> 00:00:12,543
failing in more than seventy countries.

6
00:00:12,573 --> 00:00:13,933
The early explanation?

7
00:00:13,933 --> 00:00:15,333
Not a traffic spike.

8
00:00:15,333 --> 00:00:17,273
Failed server health checks.

9
00:00:17,323 --> 00:00:18,683
Here's what that means.

10
00:00:18,683 --> 00:00:21,763
A load balancer keeps asking every server:

11
00:00:21,763 --> 00:00:22,803
are you alive?

12
00:00:22,803 --> 00:00:25,323
If one stops answering, it gets pulled

13
00:00:25,323 --> 00:00:26,634
out of rotation.

14
00:00:26,663 --> 00:00:28,823
That's perfect when one server dies.

15
00:00:28,823 --> 00:00:30,703
But when many servers fail their

16
00:00:30,703 --> 00:00:33,302
checks at once, they all get pulled.

17
00:00:33,332 --> 00:00:35,052
Now the same traffic lands on

18
00:00:35,052 --> 00:00:36,532
far fewer machines.

19
00:00:36,532 --> 00:00:38,492
They overload, slow down,

20
00:00:38,492 --> 00:00:40,241
and start failing too.

21
00:00:40,251 --> 00:00:42,904
Engineers call that a cascading failure.

22
00:00:42,954 --> 00:00:45,394
Microsoft says the final root cause

23
00:00:45,394 --> 00:00:47,573
is still being investigated.

24
00:00:47,623 --> 00:00:48,383
The lesson?

25
00:00:48,383 --> 00:00:50,703
Health checks decide who gets traffic.

26
00:00:50,703 --> 00:00:53,195
Design them to fail carefully.

